-- Task.bang — a "task" as a THUNK, not a captured continuation (ADR-0025 D1: v1 has
-- no reified continuations; a `handle` clause is implicit-tail-resume only, ADR-0095
-- D5). A cooperative coroutine that can genuinely suspend mid-body and resume later
-- at the same point needs a first-class continuation to capture — that machinery
-- does not exist on the v1 surface. `Step` is the honest v1 substitute: a task's
-- unit of work is represented as DATA, and "yielding" means returning control to the
-- driver by returning a `More` value carrying the NEXT step as a fresh thunk, rather
-- than suspending an in-flight call frame. See ../../docs/notes/sched-library-demo.md
-- §"why Step, not a continuation" for the full argument.
--
-- `Fin(u)`   — the task has finished (its result, here just `Unit`: the demos care
--              about the ORDER tasks interleave in, not a return value).
-- `More(k)`  — the task is not done; `k : Unit -> Step` is the thunk for its next
--              step. Forcing and calling `k` runs the task until its NEXT yield
--              point — exactly one step, cooperatively, then control returns to
--              whoever called `k`. The driver (main.bang) decides whether to call
--              `k` again immediately (letting one task run to completion) or to
--              round-robin across several tasks' `k`s (true interleaving) — the
--              driver's OWN loop shape is therefore also part of the "runtime",
--              alongside the Sched handler's picks (see the design note §"two
--              independent knobs").
pub data Step = Fin(Unit) | More((Unit -> Step ! {Div}))

-- makeTask tid stepsLeft: a task that yields `stepsLeft` times (via `More`), then
-- finishes. Each step is otherwise a no-op — the interleaving TRACE (which task ran
-- when, built by the driver) is the thing under test, not the task's own payload.
pub let rec makeTask : Int -> Int -> Step ! {Div} =
  fun tid => fun stepsLeft =>
    if stepsLeft == 0 then Fin(())
    else More(({fun u => ($makeTask) tid (stepsLeft - 1)} : Thunk (Unit -> Step ! {Div})))
